      "id": "0c450e7cdea68d8ea0edab8df3b372c6834b0456f03493053419075e094bce38",
      "title": "fund for investigative journalism grants 3",
      "url": "https://gfmd.info/fundings/fund-for-investigative-journalism-grants-3",
      "category": "opportunities",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T20:31:28.952Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://gfmd.info/fundings/fund-for-investigative-journalism-grants-3/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Fund for Investigative Journalism grants - GFMD",
      "pageDescription": "The Fund for Investigative Journalism provides grants and other support for reporters to produce high-quality, unbiased, nonpartisan investigative stories that have an impact.",
      "evidenceText": "Fund for Investigative Journalism grants - GFMD « Back to all Fundings Fund for Investigative Journalism grants Organisation: Fund for Investigative Journalism Status: Open Type: Grant Theme: Investigative journalism Funding Size: $10,000 - $40,000 Deadline: 29/01/2027 Eligibility: All journalists are eligible. Most grants are awarded to freelance journalists. Some journalists on staff at nonprofit and traditional media outlets also receive grants. Their investigations can be for print, online or broadcast stories, books, documentaries or podcasts. The story must also demonstrate a strong connection to the United States. Target Region: North America Application Language: English The Fund provides grants to journalists for investigative stories that break new ground – which means they uncover wrongdoing in the public and private sectors and reveal information that was previously unknown or hidden, and they shed light on accountability for wrongdoing. For regular proposals, they review proposals three to four times a year for grants up to $10,000. The next deadline is Friday, January 29, 2027. Eligibility They do not accept resubmissions of proposals that we’ve declined previously, unless we specifically invite you to resubmit. They do not accept multiple proposals from the same applicant in one grant cycle, except in rare cases. To seek an exception to this guidelines, please email us at grants@fij.org. The Board of Directors looks for: stories that break new ground and expose wrongdoing, such as corruption, malfeasance, or misuse of power – in the public and private sectors. The Fund encourages proposals written for ethnic media and submitted by journalists of color. Freelance journalists, staff reporters and media outlets are eligible for grants, and their investigations can be for print, online or broadcast stories, books, documentaries or podcasts. How to apply Applications submitted through separate application forms. Alicia Patterson Fellowships are awarded annually. Apply here . To apply and know more, please visit this page . For the latest funding opportunities, profiles of media donors, and expert insight from GFMD’s MediaDev Fundraising Guide sign up for Bottom Line newsletter Sign Up Search Search for: This website uses cookies. By continuing to use the website you consent to the use and storage of cookies on your device. You are using an outdated browser which can not show modern web content. We suggest you download Chrome or Firefox .",
